","id":50646,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:77746","name":"human metabolite","definition":"Any mammalian metabolite  produced during a metabolic reaction in humans (<em>Homo sapiens</em>).","id":77746,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35476","name":"antipsychotic agent","definition":"Antipsychotic drugs are agents that control agitated psychotic behaviour, alleviate acute psychotic states, reduce psychotic symptoms, and exert a quieting effect.","id":35476,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:76971","name":"Escherichia coli metabolite","definition":"Any bacterial metabolite produced during a metabolic reaction in <em>Escherichia coli</em>.","id":76971,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35554","name":"cardiovascular drug","definition":"A drug that affects the rate or intensity of cardiac contraction, blood vessel diameter or blood volume.","id":35554,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:23357","name":"cofactor","definition":"An organic molecule or ion (usually a metal ion) that is required by an enzyme for its activity. It may be attached either loosely (coenzyme) or tightly (prosthetic group).","id":23357,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:231911","name":"renoprotective agent","definition":"Any compound that is able to prevent damage to the kidneys.","id":231911,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:75772","name":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae metabolite","definition":"Any  fungal metabolite produced during a metabolic reaction in Baker's yeast (<em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae </em>).","id":75772,"biological_role":true},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:22587","name":"antiviral agent","definition":"A substance that destroys or inhibits replication of viruses.","id":22587,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:75835","name":"anti-anaemic agent","definition":"A compound which increases either the number of red cells or the amount of haemoglobin in the blood.","id":75835,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35623","name":"anticonvulsant","definition":"A drug used to prevent seizures or reduce their severity.","id":35623,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35526","name":"hypoglycemic agent","definition":"A drug which lowers the blood glucose level.","id":35526,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:64946","name":"anti-HIV agent","definition":"An antiviral agent that destroys or inhibits the replication of the human immunodeficiency virus.","id":64946,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:33231","name":"antitubercular agent","definition":"A substance that kills or slows the growth of <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> and is used in the treatment of tuberculosis.","id":33231,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35610","name":"antineoplastic agent","definition":"A substance that inhibits or prevents the proliferation of neoplasms.","id":35610,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:53784","name":"antispasmodic drug","definition":"A drug that suppresses spasms. These are usually caused by smooth muscle contraction, especially in tubular organs. The effect is to prevent spasms of the stomach, intestine or urinary bladder.","id":53784,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:145947","name":"antiatherosclerotic agent","definition":"A cardiovascular drug that prevents atherosclerosis (a disease in which the inside of an artery narrows due to the build up of plaque). Compare with <a href=\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:50855\" target=\"_blank\">antiatherogenic agent</a>.","id":145947,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:50919","name":"antiemetic","definition":"A drug used to prevent nausea or vomiting. An antiemetic may act by a wide range of mechanisms: it might affect the medullary control centres (the vomiting centre and the chemoreceptive trigger zone) or affect the peripheral receptors.","id":50919,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:75771","name":"mouse metabolite","definition":"Any mammalian metabolite produced during a metabolic reaction in a mouse (<em>Mus musculus</em>).","id":75771,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:50733","name":"nutraceutical","definition":"A product in capsule, tablet or liquid form that provide essential nutrients, such as a vitamin, an essential mineral, a protein, an herb, or similar nutritional substance.","id":50733,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:27314","name":"water-soluble vitamin (role)","definition":"Any vitamin that dissolves in water and readily absorbed into tissues for immediate use. Unlike the fat-soluble vitamins, they are not stored in the body and need to be replenished regularly in the diet and will rarely accumulate to toxic levels since they are quickly excreted from the body via urine.","id":27314,"biological_role":true}],"is_released":true}